<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Taizu of Liao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">872–<a href="/years/926">926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Yelü Abaoji, Abaoji · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yelü Abaoji, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Taizu</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, was the Khitan leader who founded the Liao dynasty. Chosen as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> around 907, he broke with the custom of rotating leadership, proclaimed himself emperor in 916 and built a state combining steppe and Chinese institutions, including a Khitan script. He conquered the kingdom of Balhae in 926 and died on the way home.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abaoji was born in 872 in Southern Mongolia and had a turbulent childhood. His grandfather was killed in a conflict between tribes, his father and uncles fled, and his grandmother hid him for his safety. His father had been the elected chieftain of the Yila tribe. The Yaonian clan had dominated the leadership of the Khitan tribes since the 750s, but by the end of the ninth century leaders of the Yila were expressing dissatisfaction with the Yaonian khans.</p><p>Later Chinese historians record legends of his birth: his mother, Xiao Yanmujin, dreamt that the sun fell into her bosom, and the infant was said to have the body of a three-year-old and to have walked at three months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Abaoji became chieftain of the Yila in 901 and in 903 was named Yuyue, commander of all Khitan forces, second only to the great khan. In 905 he led 70,000 cavalry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> to make a brotherhood with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a> and pledged support against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a>. At the triennial council of 907 he demanded to be named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, and with the support of seven tribal chiefs and the acquiescence of the last Yaonian great khan he became khagan on 27 February 907.</p><p>Until 916 he faced constant uprisings, mostly led by cousins and brothers, whom he won over by persuading them that they would prosper as a dynasty and by giving them posts of influence. In 916 he declared himself Celestial Emperor in the Han style, adopted an era name and named his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-bei">Yelü Bei</a> as heir apparent, which was a first among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> and contrary to their notion of rule by merit.</p><p>He introduced a dual administrative system, with steppe peoples governed by steppe tradition and the settled population of Balhae and north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by a civil bureaucracy on Han protocols. He built a walled city in 918, ordered a Khitan large script in 920, and five years later had his brother Yelü Diela devise a second, more syllabic script.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 926, while on campaign against Balhae, Abaoji demanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a> from the Later Tang envoy Yao Kun, who said he could not grant them and was imprisoned. Abaoji died of illness on 6 September 926, having captured the whole Balhae state and left its ruling family as nobles of a new fiefdom. His borders reached the Yalu and Ussuri rivers and far onto the Mongolian Plateau, and he had not acted on a plan to move south.</p><p>Although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-bei">Yelü Bei</a> was heir apparent, the empress dowager <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulu-ping">Shulü Ping</a> did not think him worthy and had her second son Deguang succeed, as Emperor Taizong. The dual system became a model for later steppe peoples.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abaoji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Khitan armies of <b>Yelü Abaoji</b> invaded in 925, Balhae's frontier fortresses and capital fell within weeks, suggesting that the kingdom's military strength had declined during the long peace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Traditionally in 907, as the Tang dynasty collapsed, <b>Abaoji</b> of the Yila tribe and the Yelü clan was chosen as khagan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Abaoji</b>, the Khitan leader later known as Emperor Taizu of Liao, became khagan of the Khitans.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-27">27 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 926 <b>Abaoji</b>, founder of the Liao dynasty, destroyed Balhae after a short campaign and took its capital Huhan (Holhan).</blockquote><a href="/state/dongdan">Dongdan</a></li></ul></section>
